3 Types of business organisations	19	Define a 'sole trader'.	A business owned and controlled by just one person.	Definition
3 Types of business organisations	20	Explain two advantages and two disadvantages of operating as a sole trader.	Advantages: Low setup costs/few legal requirements; owner keeps all profit; total control. Disadvantages: Unlimited liability; limited capital; no continuity; high workload.	4-Mark
3 Types of business organisations	21	What is 'unlimited liability'?	The owner of a business can be held personally responsible for all debts of the business; personal wealth can be seized to pay off business debts.	Definition
3 Types of business organisations	22	What is a 'partnership agreement' and what does it contain?	A written legal agreement between partners stating capital contributed, profit-sharing ratio, management responsibilities, and procedures for partners leaving or joining.	2-Mark
3 Types of business organisations	23	Explain the difference between an unincorporated business and an incorporated business.	Unincorporated: No separate legal identity between business and owner (e.g., sole trader, partnership). Incorporated: Business has a legal identity separate from its owners/shareholders (e.g., private or public limited companies).	4-Mark
3 Types of business organisations	24	What is a 'private limited company' (Ltd)?	A company owned by shareholders with limited liability, whose shares cannot be sold to the general public, usually sold only to family, friends, or private investors.	Definition
3 Types of business organisations	25	State two key differences between a Private Limited Company (Ltd) and a Public Limited Company (Plc).	Plc can sell shares to the general public on stock exchanges; Ltd cannot. 2) Plc accounts are publicly disclosed in greater detail and faces more legal formalities.	4-Mark
3 Types of business organisations	26	What is meant by the 'divorce between ownership and control'?	In large public limited companies, shareholders own the company but elected directors/managers control daily operations, which can create conflicting objectives.	2-Mark
3 Types of business organisations	27	Define 'franchise', 'franchisor', and 'franchisee'.	Franchise: Business model based on using the brand name and products of an existing company. Franchisor: Original business selling the right. Franchisee: Buyer purchasing the license to run an outlet.	4-Mark
3 Types of business organisations	28	Define a 'joint venture' and list one advantage and one disadvantage.	Joint venture: Two or more businesses agree to start a new project together, sharing capital, risks, and profits. Advantage: Shared costs/risks, local market knowledge. Disadvantage: Shared profits, possible disagreements.	4-Mark
3 Types of business organisations	29	What is a 'social enterprise'?	A business organisation that has social or environmental objectives alongside making a profit, which is reinvested back into the business or community.	Definition
4 Business objectives and stakeholder objectives	30	Define 'business objective'.	An aim or target that a business works towards achieving.	Definition
4 Business objectives and stakeholder objectives	31	State five common objectives of private sector businesses.	Survival, 2) Profit, 3) Growth, 4) Market share, 5) Service to the community.	Core Question
4 Business objectives and stakeholder objectives	32	What is 'market share' and how is it calculated?	Percentage of total market sales held by one brand or business. Formula: (Sales revenue of business / Total sales revenue for whole market) * 100.	2-Mark
4 Business objectives and stakeholder objectives	33	Explain why business objectives might change over time.	A business may focus on survival initially, then switch to profit/growth once established, or revert to survival during an economic recession.	4-Mark
4 Business objectives and stakeholder objectives	34	Distinguish between internal and external stakeholders.	Internal stakeholders work within or own the business (e.g., owners, managers, employees). External stakeholders are outside groups with a direct interest (e.g., customers, suppliers, government, local community).	4-Mark
