5 Human resource management (HRM)	36	List four main responsibilities of the Human Resources department.	Recruitment and selection, wages/salaries, industrial relations, training programmes, health and safety, redundancy and dismissal.	4-Mark
5 Human resource management (HRM)	37	Explain the difference between a 'job description' and a 'person specification'.	Job description outlines the duties and responsibilities involved in a specific job. Person specification outlines the qualifications, skills, experience, and characteristics required of the candidate.	4-Mark
5 Human resource management (HRM)	38	Define 'internal recruitment' and 'external recruitment'.	Internal recruitment: Filling a vacancy with an existing employee. External recruitment: Filling a vacancy with someone from outside the business.	4-Mark
5 Human resource management (HRM)	39	Outline two advantages and two disadvantages of internal recruitment.	Advantages: Quicker/cheaper; worker is already known; motivates staff. Disadvantages: No new external ideas; creates another vacancy; potential employee jealousy.	4-Mark
5 Human resource management (HRM)	40	What is an 'employment contract' and state three items included in it?	A legal agreement between employer and employee listing rights/responsibilities. Includes: Job title, working hours, rate of pay, holiday entitlement, notice period.	4-Mark
5 Human resource management (HRM)	41	Define 'legal minimum wage'.	The lowest hourly wage rate that employers are legally required to pay to their employees.	Definition
5 Human resource management (HRM)	42	Describe the three types of training: induction, on-the-job, and off-the-job.	Induction: Introduction for new employees to company procedures. On-the-job: Trainee observes and practices with experienced staff at the workplace. Off-the-job: Training conducted away from the workplace by specialist trainers.	6-Mark
6 Organisation and management	43	Identify the four main functional departments in a business.	Operations, Marketing, Finance, Human Resource Management.	4-Mark
6 Organisation and management	44	Define 'chain of command' and 'span of control'.	Chain of command: Structure through which authority and instructions are passed down from senior management to lower levels. Span of control: Number of subordinates working directly under a manager.	4-Mark
6 Organisation and management	45	Explain two advantages of a short chain of command and flat hierarchy.	Faster and more accurate communication; 2) Top managers are closer to lower levels; 3) Wider span of control offers more worker responsibility.	4-Mark
6 Organisation and management	46	What is 'delayering'?	Removing a whole level of management from an organisational structure to make it flatter.	Definition
6 Organisation and management	47	List the five key functions of management according to Henri Fayol.	Planning, Organising, Co-ordinating, Commanding, Controlling.	Core Question
6 Organisation and management	48	Define 'delegation' and give one benefit for managers.	Delegation: Giving subordinates authority to perform specific tasks. Benefit: Allows managers to focus on more strategic decisions.	2-Mark
6 Organisation and management	49	Compare autocratic, democratic, and laissez-faire leadership styles.	Autocratic: Manager makes all decisions without consulting workers. Democratic: Workers are consulted before final decisions are made. Laissez-faire: Broad objectives are set and workers make their own decisions.	6-Mark
6 Organisation and management	50	Differentiate between 'downsizing' and 'redundancy'.	Downsizing: Permanently reducing the overall size of the workforce. Redundancy: Loss of a job because the job itself is no longer needed by the business.	4-Mark
6 Organisation and management	51	What is a 'trade union' and list two benefits of membership for workers.	Trade union: Group of employees who join together to protect their interests. Benefits: Strength in numbers during wage negotiations; legal/financial advice; improved working conditions.	4-Mark
7 Methods of communication	52	Define 'effective communication' and list its four main features.	Transferring a message from sender to receiver, who understands and acts upon it. Features: Sender/transmitter, clear message/medium, receiver, feedback.	4-Mark
7 Methods of communication	53	Distinguish between one-way and two-way communication.	One-way: Receiver has no opportunity to reply or provide feedback. Two-way: Receiver can reply, ask questions, or provide feedback, improving understanding.	4-Mark
7 Methods of communication	54	Explain two advantages and two disadvantages of using email for business communication.	Advantages: Fast/cheap to send to many; provides written evidence; can attach detailed files. Disadvantages: Risk of information overload/spam; direct feedback not instant; technical breakdown issues.	4-Mark
7 Methods of communication	55	What is a 'communication barrier'?	Any factor that stops or disrupts the effective transmission and understanding of a message.	Definition
7 Methods of communication	56	List four causes of communication barriers.	Problems with sender (jargon, language), 2) Problems with channel (lost mail, technical failure), 3) Problems with receiver (not listening, wrong person), 4) Lack of feedback.	4-Mark
8 Motivating employees	57	Define 'motivation' and 'labour productivity'.	Motivation: Reason why employees want to work hard and effectively. Labour productivity: Output produced divided by number of workers in a given time period.	4-Mark
8 Motivating employees	58	List the five levels in Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs.	Physiological needs, 2) Safety/security needs, 3) Social needs, 4) Esteem needs, 5) Self-actualisation.	Core Question
8 Motivating employees	59	Explain Frederick Taylor's motivation theory.	Taylor assumed workers are motivated purely by money; output can be maximized by breaking jobs into simple tasks and paying workers based on piece rates.	2-Mark
8 Motivating employees	60	Differentiate between Herzberg's 'hygiene factors' and 'motivators'.	Hygiene factors (e.g., salary, working conditions) prevent job dissatisfaction but do not motivate long-term. Motivators (e.g., achievement, recognition) actively create job satisfaction and motivation.	4-Mark
