20 Costs, scale of production and break-even analysis	108	Define 'economies of scale' and list three examples.	Factors leading to a reduction in average costs as a business expands. Examples: Purchasing, marketing, financial, managerial, technical economies.	4-Mark
20 Costs, scale of production and break-even analysis	109	Define 'diseconomies of scale' and list three causes.	Factors leading to an increase in average costs as a business grows beyond a certain size. Causes: Poor communication, weak coordination, lack of worker commitment/loyalty, lack of control.	4-Mark
20 Costs, scale of production and break-even analysis	110	Define 'break-even output' and write the break-even formula using contribution.	Break-even output: Level of production where total revenue equals total costs. Formula: Fixed Costs / Contribution per unit (where Contribution = Price minus Variable Cost per unit).	2-Mark
20 Costs, scale of production and break-even analysis	111	What is meant by the 'margin of safety'?	The amount by which current output/sales exceeds the break-even point. Formula: Actual Sales minus Break-even Output.	2-Mark
21 Quality of goods and services	112	Define 'quality product'.	A product or service that meets customer expectations and is fit for its intended purpose.	Definition
21 Quality of goods and services	113	Distinguish between 'quality control' and 'quality assurance'.	Quality control: Inspecting finished products at the end of production to detect faults. Quality assurance: Setting standards at every stage of production and checking throughout to prevent faults.	4-Mark
22 Location decisions	114	List four factors that influence the location of a manufacturing business.	Land/rent costs, 2) Production methods used, 3) Proximity to market, 4) Proximity to raw materials, 5) Availability of suitable labor, 6) Government incentives.	4-Mark
22 Location decisions	115	List four factors that influence the location of a service sector business.	Proximity to customers, 2) Availability/cost of labor, 3) Rent and local taxes, 4) Competitor locations, 5) Business image, 6) Parking and access.	4-Mark
23 Business finance	116	State four main reasons why businesses need finance.	Start-up capital, 2) Expansion/growth, 3) Replacing outdated non-current assets, 4) Working capital to pay day-to-day expenses.	4-Mark
23 Business finance	117	Define 'capital expenditure' and 'revenue expenditure'.	Capital expenditure: Spending on non-current assets intended to last long-term (e.g., buildings, vehicles). Revenue expenditure: Spending on day-to-day running expenses (e.g., wages, raw materials).	4-Mark
23 Business finance	118	Define 'working capital' and write its formula.	Finance needed by a business to cover day-to-day running expenses. Formula: Current Assets minus Current Liabilities.	2-Mark
23 Business finance	119	Differentiate between internal finance and external finance.	Internal finance: Obtained from within the business or owners (e.g., retained profit, sale of assets, owner's savings). External finance: Obtained from outside providers (e.g., bank loans, overdrafts, share issues, venture capital).	4-Mark
23 Business finance	120	Identify two internal sources of finance and state one advantage for each.	Retained profit: No interest charges or repayment needed. 2) Sale of unwanted assets: Releases tied-up capital without increasing debt.	4-Mark
23 Business finance	121	Compare bank overdrafts and bank loans as sources of finance.	Bank overdraft: Short-term, flexible borrowing on a bank account; interest paid only on amount overdrawn. Bank loan: Long-term fixed sum borrowed for a set period with structured repayment and interest.	4-Mark
23 Business finance	122	Define 'leasing' and 'hire purchase'.	Leasing: Paying a regular fee to use an asset without ever acquiring ownership. Hire purchase: Buying an asset gradually via monthly installments, taking ownership after the final payment.	4-Mark
23 Business finance	123	What is 'crowdfunding'?	Raising small amounts of finance from a large number of individuals, typically organized via online platforms.	Definition
24 Cash flow forecast	124	Define 'cash inflows' and 'cash outflows' and give two examples of each.	Cash inflows: Money entering the business (e.g., cash sales, debtor payments, bank loans). Cash outflows: Money leaving the business (e.g., inventory purchases, wages, rent).	4-Mark
