Enterprise, Business Growth and Size	15	What is a Business Plan and why is it important?	A written document containing business objectives and details about operations, finance, and owners. It assists in securing finance and guiding business direction.	4-Mark
Enterprise, Business Growth and Size	16	Distinguish between Internal Growth and External Growth.	Internal growth occurs when a business expands its existing operations. External growth occurs when a business takes over or merges with another business.	4-Mark
Enterprise, Business Growth and Size	17	Explain Horizontal, Vertical, and Conglomerate integration.	Horizontal: merging/taking over a business in the same industry at the same stage of production. Vertical: merging/taking over in the same industry at a different stage of production. Conglomerate: merging/taking over a business in a completely different industry.	6-Mark
Types of Business Organisation	18	What is a Sole Trader?	A business owned and controlled by one person.	Definition
Types of Business Organisation	19	Explain the difference between Limited Liability and Unlimited Liability.	Limited liability limits shareholder liability to the amount invested. Unlimited liability means owners are personally responsible for all business debts.	4-Mark
Types of Business Organisation	20	Define a Partnership and a Partnership Agreement.	Partnership: business form where two or more people agree to jointly own a business. Partnership Agreement: written, legal agreement between business partners detailing ownership and rules.	4-Mark
Types of Business Organisation	21	Contrast Unincorporated and Incorporated businesses.	Unincorporated businesses do not have a separate legal identity from the owners (e.g. sole traders). Incorporated businesses are companies with separate legal status from their owners.	4-Mark
Types of Business Organisation	22	Compare Private Limited Companies (LTDs) and Public Limited Companies (PLCs).	Both are owned by shareholders. LTDs cannot sell shares to the general public; PLCs can sell tradeable shares to the public on the stock exchange.	4-Mark
Types of Business Organisation	23	What are Dividends?	Payments made to shareholders from company profits (after tax) as a return for investing.	Definition
Types of Business Organisation	24	Explain Franchise, Franchisee, and Franchisor.	Franchise: business based on existing brand/trading methods. Franchisee: buys licence to operate the business. Franchisor: original business selling the rights to use its brand name.	6-Mark
Types of Business Organisation	25	Define a Joint Venture.	Where two or more businesses start a new project together, sharing capital, risks, and profits.	Definition
Business Objectives and Stakeholder Objectives	26	What is a Social Enterprise?	A business with social objectives as well as an aim to make a profit to reinvest into the business.	Definition
Business Objectives and Stakeholder Objectives	27	Define Stakeholders and differentiate between Internal and External stakeholders.	Stakeholder: any person/group with a direct interest in business operations. Internal: work within/own the business (employees, managers). External: outside groups with an interest (customers, government, suppliers).	4-Mark
Recruitment, Selection and Training	28	What is Recruitment?	The process from identifying that a business needs to employ someone up to the point where applications arrive.	Definition
Recruitment, Selection and Training	29	Distinguish between a Job Description and a Job Specification (Person Specification).	Job Description outlines tasks, duties, and responsibilities of the job. Job Specification outlines qualifications, skills, physical characteristics, and experience needed by the applicant.	4-Mark
Recruitment, Selection and Training	30	Evaluate Internal Recruitment vs External Recruitment.	Internal: filling vacancies with existing staff (cheaper, known skills, but no fresh ideas). External: hiring new candidates outside the business (new skills/ideas, higher advertising costs).	6-Mark
Recruitment, Selection and Training	31	Compare On-the-job training, Off-the-job training, and Induction training.	Induction: introduces new employee to staff, procedures, and workplace. On-the-job: training at the workplace by observing experienced workers. Off-the-job: training away from workplace by specialist trainers.	6-Mark
Recruitment, Selection and Training	32	Explain the difference between Dismissal and Redundancy.	Dismissal: ending employment against worker's will due to unsatisfactory performance or contract breach. Redundancy: employee loses job because the role is no longer needed.	4-Mark
Recruitment, Selection and Training	33	What is an Industrial Tribunal?	A law court or legal meeting making judgments on employment disagreements such as unfair dismissal.	Definition
Organisation and Management	34	Explain Hierarchy, Chain of Command, and Span of Control.	Hierarchy: levels of management from highest to lowest. Chain of Command: structure passing instructions down from senior managers. Span of Control: number of subordinates working directly under a manager.	6-Mark
Organisation and Management	35	Define Delegation and give one advantage for managers.	Delegation means giving a subordinate authority to perform specific tasks. Advantage: frees up manager's time for strategic tasks.	2-Mark
Organisation and Management	36	Describe Autocratic, Democratic, and Laissez-faire Leadership Styles.	Autocratic: manager in total control, expects orders followed without discussion. Democratic: involves workers in decision-making process. Laissez-faire: sets broad goals, leaves workers to make decisions and organize work.	6-Mark
Organisation and Management	37	What is a Trade Union?	A group of employees who join together to protect their working rights and interests.	Definition
Communication	38	Explain the communication process using Sender, Receiver, Medium, and Feedback.	Sender starts the message. Medium is the method used. Receiver gets the message. Feedback is the receiver's response confirming understanding.	4-Mark
Communication	39	Distinguish between One-way and Two-way Communication.	One-way does not invite or require a response. Two-way involves feedback, discussion, and response from the receiver.	4-Mark
Communication	40	What are Communication Barriers?	Factors that prevent or stop effective communication of messages.	Definition
Motivating Employees	41	Differentiate between Wages, Salaries, Time Rate, and Piece Rate.	Wage: usually weekly pay. Salary: fixed monthly pay. Time rate: payment per hour worked. Piece rate: payment per unit produced.	4-Mark
Motivating Employees	42	Define Bonus, Commission, and Profit Sharing.	Bonus: extra payment for good work. Commission: payment linked to number of sales. Profit sharing: paying employees a share of company profits.	4-Mark
Motivating Employees	43	Explain non-financial motivators: Job Rotation, Job Enrichment, and Teamworking.	Job Rotation: swapping tasks regularly. Job Enrichment: adding higher-skill/responsibility tasks. Teamworking: allocating tasks to worker groups.	6-Mark
Marketing, Competition and the Customer	44	Define Marketing.	Identifying customer wants and satisfying them profitably.	Definition
Marketing, Competition and the Customer	45	Distinguish between a Mass Market and a Niche Market.	Mass market sells standardized items to the largest market segment. Niche market targets a small, specialized segment.	4-Mark
Marketing, Competition and the Customer	46	What is Market Segmentation?	Dividing a market into distinct sub-groups of consumers with similar characteristics or preferences.	Definition
Market Research	47	Compare Primary Market Research and Secondary Market Research.	Primary (field): collecting first-hand original data. Secondary (desk): using existing data previously collected by others.	4-Mark
Market Research	48	List four methods of Primary Market Research.	Questionnaires 2. Interviews 3. Focus groups 4. Observation	4-Mark
Marketing Mix: Product and Price	49	What are the 4 Ps of the Marketing Mix?	Product, Price, Place, and Promotion.	2-Mark
Marketing Mix: Product and Price	50	What is a Unique Selling Point (USP)?	The special feature of a product that differentiates it from competitors.	Definition
Marketing Mix: Product and Price	51	Explain the Product Life Cycle and Extension Strategies.	Product Life Cycle: stages a product passes through (Introduction, Growth, Maturity, Decline). Extension Strategy: method to maintain sales at maturity and delay decline.	4-Mark
Marketing Mix: Product and Price	52	Explain Pricing Strategies: Cost-plus, Competitive, Penetration, Price Skimming, Dynamic.	Cost-plus: cost + mark-up. Competitive: priced in line with rivals. Penetration: low price to enter market. Skimming: high price for new unique item. Dynamic: price varies with demand.	8-Mark
Marketing Mix: Place and Promotion	53	Define Channels of Distribution and list three intermediaries.	Distribution Channel: path from producer to customer. Intermediaries: Wholesalers, Retailers, Agents.	4-Mark
Marketing Mix: Place and Promotion	54	What is E-commerce?	Online buying and selling of goods and services using internet-linked devices.	Definition
Production of Goods and Services	55	Define Productivity and Labour Productivity.	Productivity measures inputs against outputs. Labour productivity is output produced per worker in a specific time frame.	4-Mark
Production of Goods and Services	56	What is Lean Production and its benefits?	Production aiming to reduce waste and cut inputs, leading to higher efficiency and reduced costs.	2-Mark
Production of Goods and Services	57	Explain Just-In-Time (JIT) inventory management.	A production method eliminating or reducing raw material/finished goods stock holdings.	Definition
Production of Goods and Services	58	What is Kaizen?	A Japanese continuous improvement philosophy focusing on waste elimination.	Definition
Production of Goods and Services	59	Compare Job, Batch, and Flow Production.	Job: single custom item made at a time. Batch: quantity of one item, then quantity of another. Flow: continuous mass production of identical items.	6-Mark
Production of Goods and Services	60	Explain CAD, CAM, and 3D Printing.	CAD: Computer-Aided Design. CAM: Computer-Aided Manufacturing via automated machinery. 3D Printing: creating 3D physical objects from digital designs.	4-Mark
Costs, Scale of Production and Break-Even Analysis	61	Define Fixed Costs and Variable Costs with examples.	Fixed costs do not change with output (e.g. rent). Variable costs vary directly with output (e.g. raw materials).	4-Mark
Costs, Scale of Production and Break-Even Analysis	62	State the formula for Total Cost and Average Cost.	Total Cost = Fixed Costs + Variable Costs. Average Cost = Total Costs / Units Produced.	2-Mark
Costs, Scale of Production and Break-Even Analysis	63	Distinguish Economies of Scale from Diseconomies of Scale.	Economies of scale reduce unit costs as scale expands. Diseconomies of scale increase average costs when business grows too large.	4-Mark
Costs, Scale of Production and Break-Even Analysis	64	Define Break-Even Point and Margin of Safety.	Break-even point: output level where Total Revenue = Total Costs. Margin of Safety: amount sales exceed the break-even output.	4-Mark
Quality Control and Assurance	65	Distinguish Quality Control from Quality Assurance.	Quality Control checks finished goods at end of production. Quality Assurance sets standards and checks quality at every production stage.	4-Mark
Financial Information and Decisions	66	What is Start-up Capital and Working Capital?	Start-up capital: finance needed to start a business. Working capital: finance needed for day-to-day operations.	4-Mark
Financial Information and Decisions	67	Compare Internal Finance and External Finance.	Internal: raised within the business (retained profit). External: obtained from outside parties (bank loans, venture capital).	4-Mark
Financial Information and Decisions	68	Define Overdrafts, Bank Loans, and Crowdfunding.	Overdraft: flexible short-term bank debt. Bank Loan: long-term set amount borrowed with interest. Crowdfunding: raising small amounts from many individuals online.	6-Mark
Financial Information and Decisions	69	What is a Cash Flow Forecast?	An estimate of future monthly cash inflows and outflows showing expected ending balances.	Definition
Financial Information and Decisions	70	State the formula for Net Cash Flow.	Net Cash Flow = Total Cash Inflows - Total Cash Outflows.	Core Question
Financial Information and Decisions	71	Distinguish between Assets and Liabilities.	Assets are items of value owned by the business. Liabilities are money/debts owed to others.	4-Mark